#!/bin/sh
# Make directly-connected subnets win over Tailscale-advertised subnet routes.
#
# THE BUG THIS FIXES
# Peers on our tailnet act as subnet routers: pilab (100.64.0.2, a Raspberry Pi
# that physically sits on the home LAN) advertises 192.168.1.0/24 AND
# 192.168.2.0/24. With `--accept-routes` (which we want -- it is what lets
# switchwrt's LAN clients reach home-LAN devices while travelling) tailscaled
# installs those prefixes into its own routing table 52 and adds
#
#	5270:	from all lookup 52
#
# which sits ABOVE the main table (32766). Whenever switchwrt is parked on a
# 192.168.2.0/24 network -- i.e. at home, which is the common case, since the
# ONT/ONT uplink hands out exactly that subnet on BOTH eth1 (wan) and wlan1
# (wwan) -- table 52's 192.168.2.0/24 shadowed our own directly-connected
# route to the very subnet we are plugged into.
#
# Observed symptom: LuCI/SSH unreachable on the WAN-side addresses. tcpdump
# showed the asymmetry perfectly -- request arrives, reply leaves the tunnel:
#
#	eth1        In   IP 192.168.2.4 > 192.168.2.16: ICMP echo request
#	tailscale0  Out  IP 192.168.2.16 > 192.168.2.4: ICMP echo reply
#
# i.e. replies to a machine one hop away on the same wire were being pushed
# into the tunnel to pilab instead of straight back out eth1. Anything that
# depends on a reply reaching a 192.168.2.0/24 host (LuCI, ssh, ping) broke,
# while LAN (192.168.3.1) and tailnet (100.64.0.21) access kept working
# because those subnets are not shadowed.
#
# THE FIX
# A rule ahead of tailscaled's `lookup 52` that consults the main table but
# SUPPRESSES its default route:
#
#	1100:	from all lookup main suppress_prefixlength 0
#
# suppress_prefixlength 0 discards any match whose prefix length is <= 0, i.e.
# the 0.0.0.0/0 default only. Net effect, by route class:
#
#   - connected subnets (192.168.2.0/24 dev eth1/wlan1, 192.168.3.0/24
#     dev br-lan) are in main -> matched here -> routed directly. FIXED.
#   - tailnet CGNAT (100.64.0.0/10) is not in main -> falls through to
#     5270 -> table 52 -> tailscale0. Unchanged.
#   - genuinely-remote advertised prefixes (192.168.1.0/24, and 192.168.2.0/24
#     when we are NOT on that subnet, e.g. travelling) are not in main
#     -> fall through to table 52 -> reachable via pilab. Unchanged, so the
#     whole point of --accept-routes still works away from home.
#   - internet traffic: main's default is suppressed here, so it falls through
#     to 5270 (table 52 has no default) and then to main at 32766, picking up
#     mwan3's metric-ordered defaults as before. Unchanged.
#
# ON THE CHOICE OF PRIORITY 1100
# The obvious placement is "just above tailscaled's rule", but tailscaled's
# priority base is NOT stable. Both of these have been observed on this box
# across reboots of the very same image:
#
#	5210/5230/5250/5270   (lookup 52 at 5270)
#	1310/1330/1350/1370   (lookup 52 at 1370)
#
# A rule pinned at 5260 therefore silently stopped working the moment
# tailscaled came back on the 13xx base -- 1370 sits ahead of 5260, so table
# 52 shadowed us again and the reply leak returned. 1100 is below both known
# bases, so it wins regardless of which one tailscaled picks, and it needs no
# re-ordering after a tailscaled restart (a dynamically-computed
# "tailscale_prio - 1" would race every restart instead).
#
# 1100 also slots in safely among mwan3's rules: mwan3's iif rules are
# 1000+index (1001 eth1, 1005 wlan1 here) so they stay ahead of us, and its
# fwmark policy rules are 2001+, i.e. behind us. Being ahead of the fwmark
# rules is harmless and in fact correct -- for internet destinations main's
# default is suppressed here so we fall through to mwan3 untouched, while for
# connected/LAN destinations we short-circuit to the direct route, which is
# what we want anyway (mwan3 has no business steering same-wire traffic).
#
# WHY A HOTPLUG SCRIPT
# The rule is independent of tailscale0 even existing, so it just needs to be
# asserted once and kept asserted. Doing it on every ifup makes it self-healing
# across boots, uplink reconnects and `mwan3 restart` (which is known to flush
# routing state here). Priority 1100 is not one of the priorities tailscaled
# manages, so its own add/cleanup passes leave it alone.
#
# Hotplug scripts are SOURCED by /sbin/hotplug-call (`. $script`), not exec'd,
# so the mode-644 that image.nix's patches mechanism gives us is fine -- no
# chmod fixup needed (unlike the real init.d scripts, see
# 93-portable-router-setup).

[ "$ACTION" = "ifup" ] || exit 0

PRIO=1100
SIG='lookup main suppress_prefixlength 0'

# $1 is the ip(8) family flag: empty for IPv4, "-6" for IPv6. Left unquoted on
# purpose so the empty case expands to nothing rather than an empty argument.
ensure_rule() {
	_fam="$1"

	# Drop any copy of this rule left at a different priority -- e.g. by an
	# older revision of this script, which pinned 5260 and was defeated when
	# tailscaled came back on its 13xx priority base.
	ip $_fam rule show 2>/dev/null |
		sed -n "s/^\([0-9][0-9]*\):.*$SIG.*/\1/p" |
		while read -r _p; do
			[ "$_p" = "$PRIO" ] && continue
			ip $_fam rule del priority "$_p" $SIG 2>/dev/null
		done

	# Idempotent: hotplug fires on every ifup, and a bare `ip rule add` would
	# otherwise stack duplicates at the same priority.
	ip $_fam rule show 2>/dev/null | grep -q "^$PRIO:.*suppress_prefixlength 0" ||
		ip $_fam rule add priority "$PRIO" $SIG
}

ensure_rule

# IPv6 has no overlapping advertised prefix today (table 52 carries only
# tailscale's own fd7a:115c:a1e0::/48), but the same shadowing would occur the
# moment a peer advertised an overlapping ULA/GUA prefix, and the idiom is
# harmless otherwise -- connected prefixes are already what we want to win.
ensure_rule -6

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PART 2: keep exit-node return traffic inside the tunnel.
#
# THE BUG THIS FIXES
# With switchwrt selected as a tailnet exit node, every stage of the path was
# correct except the very last hop. tcpdump -ni any during a live test:
#
#	tailscale0  In   100.64.0.4  > 1.1.1.1      echo request
#	eth1        Out  192.168.2.5 > 1.1.1.1      echo request   (masq -- correct)
#	eth1        In   1.1.1.1 > 192.168.2.5      echo reply
#	eth1        Out  1.1.1.1 > 100.64.0.4       echo reply     (WRONG interface)
#
# Conntrack reverses the masquerade correctly, but the resulting packet for
# 100.64.0.4 is then routed back out to the ISP instead of into tailscale0, so
# the client never sees a reply. Every exit-node request times out; DNS, ping
# and HTTPS all fail. This is 100% reproducible, not intermittent (measured
# 24/24 failures over 70s with traffic flowing the whole time).
#
# WHY
# The reply arrives on eth1, so mwan3's ingress rule matches first:
#
#	1001:	from all iif eth1 lookup 1        <- mwan3, matches
#	1005:	from all iif wlan1 lookup 5       <- mwan3, same bug on the backup
#	1100:	from all lookup main suppress_prefixlength 0
#	1370:	from all lookup 52                <- tailscale, never reached
#
# mwan3's per-uplink tables hold only that uplink's default, its own connected
# subnet and the LAN -- no 100.64.0.0/10. The default route satisfies the
# lookup, evaluation stops, and the packet leaves via the ISP. Part 1's rule at
# 1100 cannot save this either: it consults `main`, and main carries no
# 100.64.0.0/10 route at all (tailscale keeps those exclusively in table 52).
#
# Proof, before and after:
#
#	ip route get 100.64.0.4 from 1.1.1.1 iif eth1
#	  before: via 192.168.2.1 dev eth1 table 1
#	  after:  dev tailscale0 table 52
#
# THE FIX
# A destination-matched rule ahead of mwan3's ingress rules:
#
#	900:	from all to 100.64.0.0/10 lookup 52
#
# Scope is deliberately narrow -- it matches only the tailnet CGNAT range, so
# mwan3 keeps full control of every other destination and failover is
# untouched. Verified after the change: 15/15 pings OK from the first second
# (previously 0/24), HTTPS 200, and mwan3 still reports both uplinks online.
#
# ON THE CHOICE OF PRIORITY 900
# It must sit ahead of mwan3's ingress rules, which are 1000+index. 900 leaves
# room below 1000 and stays clear of Part 1 at 1100. Unlike Part 1 this rule
# cannot collide with tailscaled's own priorities -- both observed bases (13xx
# and 52xx) are far above it.
#
# ON HARDCODING TABLE 52
# 52 is tailscale's compiled-in routing table (tailscaleRouteTable), not a
# runtime-allocated number, so unlike its rule *priorities* it is stable across
# restarts. Deriving it at runtime would race tailscaled's own rule teardown on
# every restart, which is exactly the failure mode Part 1 documents.
TS_TABLE=52
TS_PRIO=900

# $1 is the ip(8) family flag (empty for IPv4, "-6" for IPv6); $2 is the tailnet
# CGNAT prefix for that family. Unquoted $_fam on purpose, as above.
ensure_ts_rule() {
	_fam="$1"
	_net="$2"

	ip $_fam rule show 2>/dev/null |
		grep -q "^$TS_PRIO:.*$_net.*lookup $TS_TABLE" ||
		ip $_fam rule add priority "$TS_PRIO" to "$_net" lookup "$TS_TABLE"
}

ensure_ts_rule '' 100.64.0.0/10

# Same reasoning for IPv6 exit-node traffic: tailscale's own ULA range lives in
# table 52 and nowhere else, so an mwan3 ingress rule would strand it
# identically the moment IPv6 is live on an uplink.
ensure_ts_rule -6 fd7a:115c:a1e0::/48

exit 0
